  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nFor much of the week ahead, longwave troughing will remain well to \nour north. A strong ridge of high pressure will continue to edge \neastward, eventually centering overhead at about 596 dam. Sounding \nclimatology out of BNA suggests about 588 dam this time of year, so \na bit of an anomalous ridge. Surface high pressure will be centered \nover the Appalachians at the same time. The hottest temperatures of \nthe forecast period appear to be Wednesday and Thursday, where the \nambient temperatures are highest, as well as an increase in \nhumidity. It's possible a Heat Advisory could be introduced for a \nfew counties later in the week, especially for portions of the mid \nand southern valley. Temperatures compared to climatology, will be \nrunning around 10 degrees above normal.\n\nA closed low aloft and subsequent shortwave is forecast to suppress \nthe ridge, sending it back west around the end of this week. At the \nsurface, a low pressure center and cold front extending out from it \nwill try to move in from the north. By the end of the first weekend \nof September, the ridge becomes centered near the Lee of the \nRockies. Moisture from the Gulf also tries to move north the same \ntime of the front edging south, which may become the next time the \nforecast area sees chances of widespread precipitation. Only the \nhigher terrain will see differential heating induced showers or \nstorm the next few afternoons. Temperatures will also trend cooler \nover the weekend and into next week, with much lower heights, \nnorthwest flow, and increasing precipitation chances. Humidity also \nexpected to drop into next week as well.",
